WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.201 --> 00:00:08.880
STEPHANIE WONG: The global
markets move billions in value

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.820
daily, creating massive
amounts of data in the process.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.160
As the volume,
variety, and complexity

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.320
of market and
alternative data expands,

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.660
financial data publishers
face mounting pressure

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.750
to modernize and deliver their
processes in an increasingly

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.790
latency-sensitive world.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.560
And consumers are
demanding more.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.570
There's a 60%
year-over-year increase

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.490
in demand for
alternative data sets.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.920
And 90% of financial
firms are forecast

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:35.280
to use the cloud for their
market data in four years.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.080
Right now, commercial
data publishers

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.900
have several ways of sharing
data sets with their customers

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:43.710
and partners, including
FTP, email, and access

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.750
to publisher APIs.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.550
These can involve
a number of steps--

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.680
hosting data sets
on a remote system,

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.500
making multiple copies of
files for each consumer,

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.270
and letting consumers
fetch the data

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:55.980
with the right entitlements.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.060
Unfortunately, this
can result in data

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:00.900
set fragmentation and
access control matrices that

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:02.550
are hard to rationalize.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:06.120
On top of that, publishers face
operational overhead managing

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.040
multiple copies of
the same data, which

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.490
becomes expensive and hard to
scale up with consumer demand,

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.980
difficulty in tracking with
whom they've shared data sets

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:15.720
and which entitlements
are granted,

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.310
and increased
innovation friction

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:20.730
from the support of legacy
processes and infrastructure.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:23.142
And it isn't easy for
consumers, either.

00:01:23.142 --> 00:01:24.600
Getting the data
from the publisher

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.400
is just the beginning
of your journey

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.080
to making that data useful.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.180
Once you fetch the data
set from publishers,

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.720
you're tasked with ingesting,
normalizing, storing,

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:36.420
and integrating it into
your own applications

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.280
and analytics pipelines.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.050
This needs specialized
skill sets,

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:42.420
and there's usually a
long fulfillment process

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.900
to retrieve and get
value out of data.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:48.120
Today, data customers
expect ease, flexibility,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.370
and cost efficiency.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.380
Datashare is an
open-source solution

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:54.420
available in the Google
Cloud Marketplace

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:58.020
to exchange commercial data
sets securely and easily.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.270
It helps organize
third-party data,

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:03.810
making it accessible and
useful to exchanges, analysts,

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:06.690
aggregators, consumers,
and fintechs.

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:10.169
With market data in the cloud,
the entire financial services

00:02:10.169 --> 00:02:12.780
ecosystem can come
together to exchange data

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.430
securely and easily.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:17.760
With Datashare, data publishers
can load and transform data

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.360
once into the cloud and entitle
it across various dimensions

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.940
from a single source to
meet customer demands

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.550
and make sharing data
sets easy and secure.

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:29.700
You can even expand your reach
by onboarding your already

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.790
licensed and entitled data sets
in the Google Cloud Marketplace

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:34.650
under the Financial
Services Solutions

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:38.070
category, where new customers
can shop for and view your data

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:38.640
sets.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:39.900
Here's how it works.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:41.850
The data publisher
loads their source data

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:44.100
into BigQuery, Google
Cloud's fully managed,

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.830
petabyte-scale,
serverless data warehouse.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:50.310
Through Datashare's fine-grained
access control policies,

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:53.070
publishers can entitle and
administer data sets and table

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:53.910
views.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:56.613
Publishers control licensing,
while Google Cloud's

00:02:56.613 --> 00:02:58.530
infrastructure takes
care of the data delivery

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:01.020
to consumers on Google Cloud.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:04.140
With Datashare's scale and
centralized policy controls,

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.450
publishers can reduce
their operational risk.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:08.760
Choose how you want to
share data through views.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.010
Instead of making copies
of the same data set,

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:13.770
you can push data sets
quickly to existing customers

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:16.855
with data exchanges made
immediately ready to query.

00:03:16.855 --> 00:03:19.230
And integrating product feedback
on the shape of the data

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.810
can be as simple as modifying
the embedded transformation

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:24.960
directives written in
SQL for your views.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.600
For consumers, data is
immediately consumable

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.700
as soon as it lands
in BigQuery, relieving

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:31.980
the toil of downloading
and ingesting data.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:34.830
You can easily access
entitled data sets by querying

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:36.750
right from BigQuery itself.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:38.700
Once you're entitled,
you can rapidly

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.590
start to get value
out of that data,

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:43.830
unlock new revenue streams,
and with less time spent

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:45.660
on data wrangling,
spend more time

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:47.460
on solving business problems.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.770
And Datashare isn't just
for financial markets.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:52.050
It can be used for
retail, health care,

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:54.330
telecommunications,
and any time you

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:56.670
want to open up new revenue
streams by distributing

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:58.380
your data at scale.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.930
To learn more, check out the
Financial Services Solutions

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:03.840
page, and stay tuned for the
next episode on selling data

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.340
on the Google Cloud Marketplace.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:08.690
[MUSIC PLAYING]

